WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.713 --> 00:00:11.107
Comparison is where the course's two-eyed thinking gets real. This page holds up to three sources next to each other, each one kept whole and attributed.

2
00:00:11.107 --> 00:00:20.382
Pick up to three sources from the list on the right. Pairing a week's Western and Indigenous reading shows both eyes on the same topic.

3
00:00:20.382 --> 00:00:29.100
Read the columns. Each column keeps the source's week, year, perspective, origin, length, and core idea visible, side by side.

4
00:00:29.100 --> 00:00:40.106
Ask for the worked weaving when you have two or more. Show a worked weaving builds one example of holding the sources together. It is the app's example of the practice, not the answer, and it hands the real question back to you.

5
00:00:40.106 --> 00:00:46.219
Keep what helps. Copy or print the weaving, or save it to your notes on this device.

6
00:00:46.219 --> 00:00:57.231
Do your own weaving. Two-Eyed Seeing asks what each reading lets you see that the other cannot. Write that in your own words; that is the thinking your assessments ask for.

7
00:00:57.231 --> 00:00:59.932
Comparisons are never graded.

8
00:00:59.932 --> 00:01:06.912
Where next: Carry the comparison into this week's reflection or your Two-Eyed Seeing Journal work.
